---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is mta sts in simple terms?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Think of it as the rulebook that a specific part of the internet follows. Every page in this section explains one rule, why it exists, and how to follow it correctly."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do I need to memorize all of this?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: bookmark this page and the related tools. The goal is understanding what to check and when, not memorizing syntax."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long does it take to implement?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Most configurations take 5-15 minutes in your DNS panel. The verification (using our tools) takes another 2 minutes."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What happens if I get it wrong?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Depends on the setting: some errors cause immediate visible failures, others silently degrade deliverability or security. Every guide includes the symptoms to watch for."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is this the same across all DNS providers?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The underlying protocol is identical; the dashboard layout and terminology differ. Our provider playbooks cover the exact flow for Cloudflare, GoDaddy, Namecheap, Route 53, and others."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where can I get help if I'm stuck?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Each guide links to the relevant free tool so you can verify your work. The support page lists email channels for questions the guides don't cover."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout title="MTA-STS: Enforcing TLS for Inbound Email" description="MTA-STS explained: the DNS-published policy that forces TLS for incoming mail, how it differs from STARTTLS, and TLS-RPT reporting."  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">MTA-STS</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">MTA-STS: Enforced TLS for Inbound Mail</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      STARTTLS upgrades an SMTP connection to TLS <em>if both sides agree</em>, which invites
      downgrade attacks: strip the handshake and mail flows in plaintext. MTA-STS closes that by
      publishing a DNS policy that says: my mail servers demand valid TLS, always.
    </p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Two pieces. First, a TXT record at <code>_mta-sts.yourdomain.com</code> announcing that a
        policy exists and its version. Second, the policy itself served over HTTPS at
        <code>https://mta-sts.yourdomain.com/.well-known/mta-sts.txt</code>, listing your MX hosts
        and the mode: <code>testing</code> (report only) or <code>enforce</code> (refuse
        non-TLS delivery). Sending servers fetch the policy, verify the certificate, and only
        deliver over trustworthy TLS.
      </p>
      <h2>The record</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">_mta-sts.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=STSv1; id=2026082201"</pre>
      <p>
        The <code>id</code> changes to push policy updates. Keep a valid HTTPS cert on the policy
        host; an unreachable policy host weakens the whole mechanism.
      </p>
      <h2>TLS-RPT: the reports that make it manageable</h2>
      <p>
        Its companion record (<code>_smtp._tls</code>) tells providers where to email delivery
        failures: which senders could not do TLS, which certificates failed. Roll out in testing
        mode with reports for a few weeks, then enforce. Our homepage scanner checks both records.
      </p>
      <h2>Who needs it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Anyone receiving sensitive mail: finance, healthcare, legal.</li>
        <li>Compliance frameworks that name transport encryption.</li>
        <li>Organizations completing the full authentication stack: SPF, DKIM, <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a>, MTA-STS.</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
